// khuStdCollection -- the array substrate.
//
// A Khudra `Array<T>` is a fixed-length, garbage-collected block of elements.
// It is the one container the runtime knows about; everything with a growth
// policy is written on top of it in Khudra.
//
// ## Creating one
//
// `arrayCreate` is the second member in the standard library whose first
// argument is a *type*, and it is one for the same reason `khuStdMath.convertTo`
// is: the element type is the whole point of the call, and Khudra has no way to
// write a type as a parameter. So it is a compiler intrinsic rather than a
// declaration, it lowers to the `arraynew` instruction, and it cannot appear in
// this file:
//
//     Array<int32> counts = khuStdCollection.arrayCreate(int32, 8);
//     Array<string> names = khuStdCollection.arrayCreate(string, 3);
//
// A fresh array holds its element type's default: 0 for a number, false for a
// bool, `null` -- not instantiated yet -- for a reference.
//
// ## Reading and writing
//
// `[]` reads and writes an element, and it is the language's, not this
// namespace's:
//
//     counts[0] = 5;
//     io.printLine(counts[0]);
//
// An index outside the array is a **fatal runtime error**, not a sentinel.
// That is deliberate and it is the opposite of the choice `khuStdString.charAt`
// makes: a string index reaches into data, where being out of range is an
// ordinary answer, while an array index reaches into storage the program
// itself sized, where being out of range is a bug in the program.
//
// ## Erasure
//
// `Array<T>` is checked, not reified: the compiler knows the element type, the
// runtime does not. An array is a block of tagged values at run time, whatever
// the source said it held. Two consequences worth knowing:
//
//   - A bare `Array` -- the untyped form that predates generics, and what
//     `khu.getType()` still returns -- is the same runtime thing. It converts
//     to and from any `Array<T>` freely, and it cannot be indexed, because
//     indexing needs an element type and it has not got one.
//   - Arrays are always garbage collected. A manual object *stored in* one is
//     pinned for as long as the slot holds it, the same way a field pins one.

// ## Hashing an erased element
//
// A container over a `T` can do exactly two things to what it holds: ask what
// bucket it belongs in, and ask whether two of them are the same. Neither can
// be *declared* here, because both take a value of any type and Khudra has no
// way to write that -- so, like `arrayCreate`, the compiler recognizes them:
//
//     khuStdCollection.hash(value) -> int32       always non-negative
//     khuStdCollection.sameValue(a, b) -> bool
//
// Both are **deterministic**, which matters more than it looks: a `Map`'s
// layout is decided by them, so if they varied by run or by backend, so would
// its iteration order, and the byte-identical invariant would be a matter of
// luck. A reference therefore hashes to 0 rather than to its address, which
// means an object-keyed `Map` degrades to a linear scan. That is the price of
// an answer that does not depend on where the allocator put things.
//
// `sameValue` compares numbers and bools by their **bits**, so -0.0 is not 0.0
// and a NaN is itself. That is the rule a hash table needs -- equal keys must
// hash equally -- and hashing the same bits is what makes it hold.

public namespace khuStdCollection {

    // How many elements `array` holds. Accepts any array, typed or not:
    // a length does not depend on the element type.
    native func arraySize(Array array) -> int32;

    // Every element of `items` in order, with `separator` between them. An
    // empty list joins to "", and a list of one joins to that one element with
    // no separator at all.
    //
    // This is where a program goes once `khuStdString.concat` runs out of
    // arity: a response body, a PATH-like list, a comma-separated line. It
    // reads the list through its own `size()` and `get()`, so a `List` that
    // holds something other than strings is a runtime error rather than
    // nonsense -- generics are erased, and this is the point where that has to
    // be noticed.
    native func join(List<string> items, string separator) -> string;
}
